.TH "bathalign" 1 "@BATH_DATE@" "BATH @BATH_VERSION@" "BATH Manual"

.SH NAME
bathalign \- align sequences to a profile HMM


.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bathalign
[\fIoptions\fR]
.I hmmfile
.I seqfile


.SH DESCRIPTION

.PP
.B bathalign
performs a multiple sequence alignment of all the sequences in
.I seqfile
by aligning each of them individually to the profile HMM in
.IR hmmfile ,
and writes the resulting alignment to stdout.

.PP
Unlike
.BR bathsearch ,
.B bathalign
does not translate or search across reading frames: the sequences in
.I seqfile
must already be in the same alphabet as the profile in
.IR hmmfile .
For an amino acid profile, this means
.I seqfile
should contain protein sequences, not DNA.

.PP
.I hmmfile
should contain only a single profile; if it contains more, bathalign
fails with an error rather than guessing which one to use. It may be
either a BATH-formatted profile
.RB ( .bhmm ,
as produced by
.BR bathbuild ),
or a plain HMMER3-formatted profile
.RB ( .hmm ).
Because
.B bathbuild
only builds amino acid profiles, a DNA or RNA profile must come from
HMMER's
.BR hmmbuild .

.PP
Either
.I hmmfile
or
.I seqfile
(but not both) may be '\-' (dash), which means reading that input from
stdin rather than a file.

.PP
The sequences in
.I seqfile
are aligned in unihit local alignment mode. Therefore they should
already be known to contain only a single domain (or a fragment of
one). The optimal alignment may assign some residues as nonhomologous,
in which case these residues are still included in the resulting
alignment, but shoved to the outer edges. To trim these unaligned
residues from the result, see the
.B \-\-trim
option.


.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.B \-h
Help; print a brief reminder of command line usage and all available
options.

.TP
.BI \-o " <f>"
Direct the output alignment to file
.IR <f> ,
rather than to stdout.

.TP
.BI \-\-mapali " <f>"
Merge the existing alignment in file
.I <f>
into the result, where
.I <f>
is exactly the same alignment that was used to build the model in
.IR hmmfile .
This is done using a map of alignment columns to consensus profile
positions that is stored in
.IR hmmfile .
The multiple alignment in
.I <f>
will be exactly reproduced in its consensus columns (as defined by the
profile), but the displayed alignment in insert columns may be
altered, because insertions relative to a profile are considered by
convention to be unaligned data.

.TP
.B \-\-trim
Trim nonhomologous residues from the resulting multiple alignment
output.

.TP
.B \-\-amino
Assert that sequences in
.I seqfile
and the profile in
.I hmmfile
are protein, bypassing alphabet autodetection.

.TP
.B \-\-dna
Assert that sequences in
.I seqfile
and the profile in
.I hmmfile
are DNA, bypassing alphabet autodetection.

.TP
.B \-\-rna
Assert that sequences in
.I seqfile
and the profile in
.I hmmfile
are RNA, bypassing alphabet autodetection.

.TP
.BI \-\-informat " <s>"
Assert that input
.I seqfile
is in format
.IR <s> ,
bypassing format autodetection. Common choices for
.I <s>
include
.BR fasta ,
.BR embl ,
and
.BR genbank .
Alignment formats also work; common choices include
.BR stockholm ,
.BR a2m ,
.BR afa ,
.BR psiblast ,
.BR clustal ,
and
.BR phylip .
The string
.I <s>
is case-insensitive
.RB ( fasta
or
.B FASTA
both work).

.TP
.BI \-\-outformat " <s>"
Write the output alignment in format
.IR <s> .
Common choices for
.I <s>
include
.BR stockholm ,
.BR a2m ,
.BR afa ,
.BR psiblast ,
.BR clustal ,
and
.BR phylip .
The string
.I <s>
is case-insensitive
.RB ( a2m
or
.B A2M
both work). The default is
.BR stockholm .


.SH SEE ALSO

.BR bathsearch (1),
.BR bathbuild (1),
.BR bathfetch (1),
.BR bathconvert (1),
.BR bathstat (1),
.BR bathemit (1)

.PP
For complete documentation, see the BATH repository:
https://github.com/TravisWheelerLab/BATH


